I was just thinking ... You can logically link one tense cmavo with an
other tense cmavo, making the sumtcita filling the two place, and we
can understand it as if the modal place resulting of theses linked
tense cmavo was something more than just the separate tenses place
with an assertion that they are true both, or neither, and so on. Like
a tanru : la'e lu mi prami je se censa do li'u ka'e stidi lo lijda
zatpa'i
even if my example is not the best because a simple tanru can also do
the job, or you can use {jdapa'i} to convey this specific meaning.
But the essential is here to suggest.
ta'onai I wondered : why can't we (at least I never notice this usage)
write things like that : mi fijefe'eca'o do cabyfau
would it not be a good poetic tips pe'ipei ?

pe'i it would be necessary to modify the grammar unless it is a pure
morphologic question, so it was innately impossible.

